# kills: the landscape deck strip scrolls rather than clips, at both ends
# suite: node --test --test-name-pattern ^the.landscape.deck.strip.scrolls.rather.than.clips..at.both.ends$ tests/e2e.test.js
# The landscape h1 goes from visually-hidden back to a drawn 14px title that
# also claims fixed width. This is the defect the landscape block's own
# comment describes and then claims to have fixed: "with the title out the
# strip gets the whole width, and on the two WIDER rows it then fits".
#
# Re-targeted 2026-09-28 (lane M1, menu-shell): the original mutant only
# dropped the visually-hidden rule (leaving h1 a shrink-to-fit flex item, its
# width now governed by `.hrow{display:contents}`), which no longer crowds
# either wide row enough to overflow - the settings-trigger swap freed extra
# width the modebar used to occupy, and a bare font-size bump costs the row
# only as many pixels as the title text itself needs. A forced width is what
# actually reproduces "the title takes real space back from the strip"; 300px
# reliably pushes 926x428 and 1280x500 over their own content width, which is
# the wide-row assertion this mutant exists to kill. The header-height and
# chrome-budget assertions next door do not move: the row is still one 44px
# line, because the title sits INSIDE it.
diff --git a/index.html b/index.html
--- a/index.html
+++ b/index.html
@@ -310,12 +310,11 @@ h1{font-family:Marcellus, serif; font-weight:400; font-size:clamp(20px,5.5vw,27p
   /* Visually hidden, NOT display:none: the h1 stays in the accessibility
      tree, so the document keeps its heading and the header keeps its name.
      The clip-rect pair is the old and the current spelling of the same
      thing; both are here because Safari honoured clip long before it
      honoured clip-path. */
-  h1{position:absolute; width:1px; height:1px; margin:0; padding:0; border:0;
-    overflow:hidden; white-space:nowrap; clip:rect(0 0 0 0); clip-path:inset(50%)}
+  h1{width:300px; font-size:14px}
   /* The strip takes what is left. min-width:0 is what lets it: a flex item's
      automatic minimum is its content, so without it the strip would refuse to
      shrink and push the settings trigger off the row. */
   .decks{flex:1 1 auto; min-width:0}
   /* The h1 row's grid is a portrait-only device (it exists to centre the
